WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.880
Administrative access is one of the highest-value parts of the network.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.780
Administrators can change firewall rules, routing, VPN profiles, wireless settings,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.440
DNS zones, DHCP scopes, device firmware, and logging destinations.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.180
Because the access is powerful, it should be tightly controlled.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.360
Shared administrator accounts, default passwords, broad VPN access,

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:29.780
and exposed management interfaces create avoidable risk and make accountability difficult.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.240
Good administrative access starts with unique accounts, role separation,

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.500
multi-factor authentication, and least privilege.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.440
A help desk account, network engineering account, firewall administration account,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.260
and emergency break-glass account should not all have the same permissions.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.320
Privilege should match the job, and elevated access should be reviewed.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:55.100
Session logging and change records help teams understand who changed what and when.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.440
Remote administration should use controlled access paths.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:03.620
That may include a VPN, bastion host, jump host, management network,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.160
or other approved administrative pathway.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:11.740
The important properties are authentication, authorization, logging, and limited reach.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.280
Administrative interfaces should not be exposed broadly just because it is convenient.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.860
Convenience that bypasses monitoring and access control often becomes operational debt.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.400
Emergency access also needs design.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:25.980
During an outage,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.160
administrators may need a protected way to recover systems even when normal identity or

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.220
network services are degraded.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.920
Those procedures should be documented, tested, and protected with strong controls.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.020
Secure administration balances restriction with resilience:

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.160
only the right people should have access,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.740
and they should still be able to perform authorized recovery safely.
